1
00:00:01,139 --> 00:00:05,179
Most deployment failures happen because of a human error or a

2
00:00:05,179 --> 00:00:07,869
configuration drift between the environments.

3
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:13,539
I'm going to show you how to eliminate those risks by implementing a fully

4
00:00:13,539 --> 00:00:17,979
automated continuous delivery pipeline using Google Cloud Deploy,

5
00:00:18,059 --> 00:00:20,100
Skaffold, and Kustomize.

6
00:00:20,209 --> 00:00:24,219
Let's go to our cloud console. Now here what I have done

7
00:00:24,219 --> 00:00:27,319
is I have created the two clusters.

8
00:00:27,459 --> 00:00:33,959
If I go down to our Kubernetes engine and if I show you the clusters there,

9
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:36,169
here we have two clusters.

10
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:38,090
One is dev‑cluster.

11
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,119
Another one is a prod‑cluster.

12
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:43,779
Also, I have created a sample Flask app.

13
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,139
Let me show you that.

14
00:00:45,159 --> 00:00:47,880
I have created a folder, my‑app‑repo.

15
00:00:47,959 --> 00:00:51,470
Inside that folder, I have my main.py.

16
00:00:51,470 --> 00:00:56,470
In the main.py, you can see that we have a sample

17
00:00:56,470 --> 00:00:59,759
application of Flask that is just saying hello.

18
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:05,449
Now we want this Flask application to behave differently in

19
00:01:05,449 --> 00:01:08,510
dev than what it does in the production.

20
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:11,980
Now rather than me doing a manual tweak,

21
00:01:12,169 --> 00:01:16,760
we're going to let our pipeline handle the environment logic,

22
00:01:16,849 --> 00:01:20,989
the container scaling, and the deployment safely all through the code.

23
00:01:21,625 --> 00:01:26,525
The brain of this setup is the customize. Look at our base folder.

24
00:01:27,065 --> 00:01:28,625
Now inside this base folder,

25
00:01:28,754 --> 00:01:34,135
it has a generic blueprint like deployment.yaml file that tells you

26
00:01:34,555 --> 00:01:38,355
what is the name of the app and other information,

27
00:01:38,595 --> 00:01:42,665
and we have the kustomization.yaml that contains the

28
00:01:42,665 --> 00:01:46,135
deployment.yaml and service.yaml information.

29
00:01:46,275 --> 00:01:49,055
Important thing here is the overlays.

30
00:01:49,559 --> 00:01:53,209
Under this overlays folder, I have created two folders.

31
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:56,330
One is for the dev environment and another one is

32
00:01:56,330 --> 00:01:57,809
for the production environment.

33
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:02,970
And inside the dev, we have the kustomization.yaml that is

34
00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:05,730
specific for the development environment.

35
00:02:05,889 --> 00:02:09,570
We are telling Kubernetes to keep it simple here

36
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:11,929
into our development environment.

37
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:17,809
However, in the production environment, I'm specifically saying that Kubernetes,

38
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:22,410
please have the two replicas because I'm setting here the count as 2.

39
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:26,570
So when it deploys in the development environment, it creates one replica.

40
00:02:26,929 --> 00:02:30,869
When it deploys in the production, it will create the two replicas.

41
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:35,570
And here in the clouddeploy.yaml file, you can see

42
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:38,119
that we have created a pipeline,

43
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:41,850
and that pipeline is saying that it has two stages.

44
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:45,600
At line number 7 and at line number 9,

45
00:02:45,699 --> 00:02:50,660
you can see that we will have one development stage and one is

46
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:54,639
the production stage. And at line number 17,

47
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:59,470
I'm giving the cluster information about the dev cluster, and at line

48
00:02:59,470 --> 00:03:04,080
number 24, I'm giving the cluster information about the production

49
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:07,360
cluster. And if I show you this skaffold.yaml,

50
00:03:07,479 --> 00:03:14,000
now skaffold.yaml contains all the information about the path for the

51
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,710
development environment and for the production environment.

52
00:03:16,839 --> 00:03:18,800
You can look at the line number 14.

53
00:03:18,990 --> 00:03:22,639
It is showing the development environment‑related

54
00:03:22,639 --> 00:03:26,330
information and line number 18 is showing where it can find

55
00:03:26,669 --> 00:03:28,789
the production‑related information.

56
00:03:28,949 --> 00:03:32,149
Now we're going to use this skaffold to build our

57
00:03:32,149 --> 00:03:35,770
container image, and later on we're going to use Cloud

58
00:03:35,770 --> 00:03:38,880
Deploy to deploy that specific build.

59
00:03:38,979 --> 00:03:42,160
So let's just open the terminal. Now I'm scaffold

60
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:44,570
build command to build the artifacts.

61
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:48,860
Let me first get into my folder, that is my‑app‑repo.

62
00:03:48,970 --> 00:03:52,020
Inside my folder, I'm going to run the skaffold

63
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:54,100
build command saying skaffold build.

64
00:03:54,259 --> 00:03:59,460
Now it is going to build an image for us. So you can see that our build has

65
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:03,529
been successfully created and image has been pushed as well.

66
00:04:03,699 --> 00:04:06,440
Now it's time to go ahead and create the pipeline.

67
00:04:06,619 --> 00:04:08,059
So for pipeline creation,

68
00:04:08,339 --> 00:04:12,929
I'm going to run this simple command saying gcloud deploy,

69
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:17,220
and I already have delivery‑pipeline.yaml that contains all the

70
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:19,809
information like we should have two stages.

71
00:04:20,029 --> 00:04:23,049
One is development and the second one is production.

72
00:04:23,269 --> 00:04:24,579
So let me hit the Enter,

73
00:04:24,589 --> 00:04:28,429
and this is going to create a pipeline which will have two stages.

74
00:04:28,549 --> 00:04:31,489
Now it seems like the pipeline has been created.

75
00:04:31,790 --> 00:04:36,350
Let's just go to the Cloud Deploy and see if we have the pipeline ready.

76
00:04:36,589 --> 00:04:39,250
So I go down to the Cloud Deploy, and there you can

77
00:04:39,250 --> 00:04:42,200
see that we got a flask‑pipeline.

78
00:04:42,459 --> 00:04:46,980
If I click on this flask‑pipeline, you can see we have two stages.

79
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:50,130
One is for dev, and another one is the prod.

80
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:52,779
So our pipeline got created successfully.

81
00:04:52,899 --> 00:04:55,799
Now it's time to go ahead and create the release.

82
00:04:56,140 --> 00:05:00,339
Once you create the release, it will deploy to the dev environment,

83
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:03,940
and from there on we're going to promote it to the production environment.

84
00:05:04,059 --> 00:05:05,920
So let's go back to the console,

85
00:05:06,019 --> 00:05:09,929
and this time I'm going to write a command to create the release.

86
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:12,970
So I'm running a command gcloud deploy release.

87
00:05:13,290 --> 00:05:14,859
This is going to create the release,

88
00:05:15,089 --> 00:05:20,609
and I'm going to give the name as release‑0001 and delivery‑pipeline,

89
00:05:20,730 --> 00:05:22,890
and I'm giving my region as well.

90
00:05:23,010 --> 00:05:24,290
Let's hit Enter.

91
00:05:24,489 --> 00:05:27,549
Now you can see that it has created the release.

92
00:05:27,929 --> 00:05:31,130
Now if we go back to our Cloud Deploy,

93
00:05:31,369 --> 00:05:34,549
go to the delivery pipelines, now here you can see that

94
00:05:34,890 --> 00:05:39,519
release‑0001 is there. You can click on that release, and you

95
00:05:39,519 --> 00:05:41,779
can see that it is in the progress.

96
00:05:41,970 --> 00:05:44,910
It will take a while and once everything goes right,

97
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:49,309
you will see a green mark here saying that it has deployed

98
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:51,040
successfully into the dev environment.

99
00:05:51,279 --> 00:05:52,739
Meanwhile, it is in progress.

100
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:55,510
If you want to see the logs, you can click on that,

101
00:05:55,519 --> 00:06:00,290
and here you can keep on seeing all the logs on the right‑hand side column.

102
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:04,010
Now great, you can see that it has deployed successfully,

103
00:06:04,070 --> 00:06:06,750
and we are seeing the green mark. Now, let me go back

104
00:06:06,750 --> 00:06:10,730
to the release. And in this release, if on these three dots,

105
00:06:10,829 --> 00:06:11,540
if I click,

106
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:15,510
I can see the View rollouts, which give me all the information it has

107
00:06:15,510 --> 00:06:19,649
used in the manifest file and the scaffold.yaml.

108
00:06:19,790 --> 00:06:23,829
Now you can go to the pipeline, and here you can see that

109
00:06:23,829 --> 00:06:27,959
we have the deployment done up to dev completely. And if

110
00:06:27,959 --> 00:06:29,899
this looks perfectly fine,

111
00:06:30,119 --> 00:06:34,040
you can click on the Promote, and under the Promote, you can check here

112
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:38,470
Manifest difference. And you can see that it is saying that when it is

113
00:06:38,470 --> 00:06:41,250
going to do the deployment at line number 33,

114
00:06:41,279 --> 00:06:45,320
the replicas it is going to create is two because we have

115
00:06:45,320 --> 00:06:49,399
written in our production overlays file saying that in the

116
00:06:49,399 --> 00:06:51,730
production we want are two copies of it.

117
00:06:51,829 --> 00:06:55,230
Now you can say that promote, and now the deployment for the

118
00:06:55,230 --> 00:06:57,609
production gets started. And if everything goes fine,

119
00:06:58,190 --> 00:07:01,950
this will get successfully deployed into the production environment as well.

120
00:07:02,190 --> 00:07:02,470
Done.

121
00:07:02,630 --> 00:07:05,510
So you can see that we have successfully pushed our

122
00:07:05,510 --> 00:07:07,309
code into the production as well.

123
00:07:07,470 --> 00:07:11,369
That's how you can move your code from dev to the prod

124
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,309
without doing any manual intervention.
